Five questions somebody will ask, and the query that answers each.

  what did we spend today, per tenant
      meridian  4  0.00135

  how often do we refuse
      1  4

  which questions get asked twice
      What was revenue in 2024 Q3?  2

  what did the run the user complained about do
      Why did margin fall in 2025 Q1?  query_warehouse  2.9
      Why did margin fall in 2025 Q1?  search_documents  1.4

  did the suite pass on the commit we shipped
      deterministic  0.92  True

  tables: documents, evaluations, feedback, runs, steps
  file:   data/meridian/clarity.db (80 KB)

None of those five questions can be answered from a log file, and all five
get asked. That is the whole argument for a database here — not scale, which
Clarity does not have, but *joins*: a complaint has to reach the run, the run
has to reach its steps, and the deployment has to reach the eval that passed
before it went out.

Three decisions in that schema are worth arguing about now rather than later.

Every table carries a tenant. Chapter 26 enforces it; the schema only has to
make it possible, and retrofitting a tenant column onto a live system is a
migration nobody enjoys twice.

Steps store arguments and result *sizes*, not results. §23.5's reasoning about
what belongs in a trace applies to a database you own as much as to a vendor
you do not — with one difference: here you can afford a separate table with
its own retention if you decide you need the text.

And the run carries the trace id. Without it the database and the tracing
backend are two accounts of the same afternoon that cannot be joined, which is
the single most common gap between a system that has observability and a
system that has dashboards.
